{"name":"Festive Ferret","hex":"#dfdfe5","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"festive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the atmosphere, decoration, or attitude of a festival, holiday, or celebration.","In the mood to celebrate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/festive"},{"term":"ferret","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long-bodied domesticated mammal of the species Mustela furo, descended from the European polecat and often trained to hunt burrowing animals.","A black-footed ferret (Mustela nigripes)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt game with ferrets.","To uncover and bring to light by searching; usually to ferret out."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ferret"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ferret","blocks":[{"text":"The ferret is a small, domesticated species belonging to the family Mustelidae. The ferret is most likely a domesticated form of the wild European polecat, as evidenced by the ferret's ability to interbreed with European polecats and produce hybrid offspring. Physically, ferrets resemble other mustelids because of their long, slender bodies. Including their tail, the average length of a ferret is about 50 cm (20 in); they weigh between 0.7 and 2.0 kg ; and their fur can be black, brown, white, or a mixture of those colours. The species is sexually dimorphic, with males being considerably larger than females."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Ferret_2008.png/330px-Ferret_2008.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferret","wikidata":"Q178973"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Ferret_2008.png/500px-Ferret_2008.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Ferret_2008.png/1280px-Ferret_2008.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut